--- field object.package.name
type: str

The name of the package the event is about. A package's name is its
identity across every repository that serves it (PSPU §5.3), so the same
package from two repositories has the same value here;
`source.repository.name` says which one an install drew from.

A transaction that touches several packages writes one record for each, so
every record names at most one package.

--- field object.package.version
type: str

The version of the package an event is about: the version installed, the
version an upgrade or downgrade moved to, or the version an uninstall
removed. A version of PSPU §5.5, compared by PSPU §5.6.

--- field object.package.version-previous
type: str

The version a package moved from, beside `object.package.version`, on an
upgrade, downgrade or undo. Absent on a fresh install and on an uninstall,
which replace no version.

A `-previous` variant, which PGSS §6.4 permits without a definition. It is
defined anyway so that the Events book generator, which does not apply that
allowance, can resolve it.

--- field object.package.architecture
type: str

The architecture of the package an event is about: an identifier of PSPU
§5.8, such as `x86_64`, `aarch64` or `noarch`. `noarch` is a real
architecture, not the absence of one. Absent when the plan did not carry
one, as for a package being uninstalled.

--- field source.repository.name
type: str

The repository a package was installed or upgraded from, by the name it is
configured under. One transaction can draw from several repositories, which
is why this is per package. It is how a bad install is traced back to the
repository that served it.

Absent when the package came from a local file, which is the only way an
install or upgrade has no repository. An uninstall has no source and never
carries it. The role is `source` because the repository is where the
package came from; `object.repository.name` is the field for events about a
repository itself.

--- field object.repository.name
type: str

The repository the event is about, by the name it is configured under.
The name is local configuration, not an identity: a repository is
identified by its base URL, in `object.repository.url`, and two systems
may call one repository by different names.

--- field object.repository.url
type: str

The base URL of the repository the event is about, which is what
identifies it (PSPU §5.36). An HTTPS URL unless the repository was
given an insecure-transport allowance, so an `http:` value here marks a
repository whose indexes are fetched without transport security.

--- field object.claim.role
type: str

The role a claim event changed the holder of. A role is a virtual name
that several installed packages may contend to own on the filesystem,
with at most one holding it at a time (PSPU §5.23).

--- field object.claim.holder
type: str

The package that holds `object.claim.role` after the event, by name.
Absent when the event left the role unheld, as revoking a claim does.

--- event peipkg.package.installed
tier: essential
gating: none — every installation is recorded
cardinality: once per package a transaction installs; once per named package on a rejected request

The record that peipkg installed a package, or tried to and failed. A
transaction writes one record for each package its plan installs, whichever
command ran it: an `upgrade` that pulls in a new dependency writes this
event for the dependency, and undoing an uninstall writes it for the
package restored. A request refused before any transaction opened writes
one failed record for each package it named.

Essential because a package installation changes what the system runs, and
it is rare. Replaces `peipkg.install`, and the install half of
`peipkg.transaction-failed`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
  The operator: the user SID of the token peipkg ran under. This is
  peipkg's own word; the kernel-stamped `emitter.token.guid` is the
  identity it cannot forge.
field: transaction.id                  optional
  The peipkg transaction, for every record of one transaction. Absent when
  the request was refused before a transaction opened, or when a cross-root
  transaction failed while preparing its roots. In a cross-root
  transaction, the transaction of the root this package is installed in.
field: object.package.name             optional
  Absent only on a refused request that named no package.
field: object.package.version          optional
  The version installed. Absent on a refused request that named no version.
field: object.package.architecture     optional
field: source.repository.name          optional
  Absent for an install from a local file.
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.reason                  when outcome.success == false
  values: stale | busy | denied | unowned | alternate-upgrade | unresolvable | untrusted | failed
  closed: false
  peipkg's stable error code for the failure, the one its driven mode
  reports. Whether anything was rolled back follows from
  `transaction.id`: a failure with one was rolled back, a failure without
  one was refused before it began.
field: outcome.detail                  optional
  The error as peipkg reported it to the operator. Absent on success.

--- event peipkg.package.upgraded
tier: essential
gating: none — every upgrade, downgrade and undo is recorded
cardinality: once per package a transaction moves to another version; once per named package on a rejected request

The record that peipkg moved an installed package to another version, or
tried to and failed. A downgrade and an undo are recorded here too:
comparing `object.package.version` with `object.package.version-previous`
by PSPU §5.6 tells which way the package moved. A refused downgrade or undo
is recorded here as well, with the version it asked for.

Replaces `peipkg.upgrade`, and the upgrade half of
`peipkg.transaction-failed`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: transaction.id                  optional
  Absent when the request was refused before a transaction opened, or when
  a cross-root transaction failed while preparing its roots.
field: object.package.name             optional
  Absent only on a refused request that named no package, such as an
  upgrade of everything.
field: object.package.version          optional
  The version the package moved to.
field: object.package.version-previous optional
  The version it moved from. Absent on a refused request.
field: object.package.architecture     optional
field: source.repository.name          optional
  Absent for an upgrade from a local file.
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.reason                  when outcome.success == false
  values: stale | busy | denied | unowned | alternate-upgrade | unresolvable | untrusted | failed
  closed: false
field: outcome.detail                  optional

--- event peipkg.package.uninstalled
tier: essential
gating: none — every uninstallation is recorded
cardinality: once per package a transaction removes; once per named package on a rejected request

The record that peipkg removed a package, or tried to and failed. A
transaction writes one record for each package its plan removes, including
a package removed to resolve a conflict during an install, and a package
removed by undoing its install.

Replaces `peipkg.uninstall`, and the uninstall half of
`peipkg.transaction-failed`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: transaction.id                  optional
  Absent when the request was refused before a transaction opened.
field: object.package.name             required
field: object.package.version          optional
  The version removed. Absent on a refused request.
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.reason                  when outcome.success == false
  values: stale | busy | denied | unowned | alternate-upgrade | unresolvable | untrusted | failed
  closed: false
field: outcome.detail                  optional

--- event peipkg.claim.changed
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per claim grant or revoke

The record that peipkg granted a role to a package or revoked it, or tried
to and failed. A grant names the new holder; a revoke leaves
`object.claim.holder` out. A change the operator declines at the proceed
prompt writes nothing.

Replaces `peipkg.claim`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: transaction.id                  optional
field: object.claim.role               required
field: object.claim.holder             optional
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.reason                  when outcome.success == false
  values: stale | busy | denied | unowned | alternate-upgrade | unresolvable | untrusted | failed
  closed: false
field: outcome.detail                  optional

--- event peipkg.repository.added
tier: essential
gating: none — every repository trust ceremony is recorded
cardinality: once per repository add

The record that peipkg added a repository and established trust in it, or
tried to and failed. Adding a repository decides whose packages the system
will install, which is why it is essential. A command refused before it
names a base URL, such as `repo add <name>` for a repository with no
configuration, writes nothing.

Replaces `peipkg.repo-add`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: object.repository.name          required
field: object.repository.url           required
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.reason                  when outcome.success == false
  values: stale | busy | denied | unowned | alternate-upgrade | unresolvable | untrusted | failed
  closed: false
field: outcome.detail                  optional
  The error as peipkg reported it to the operator. Absent on success.

--- event peipkg.repository.removed
tier: essential
gating: none — every repository removal is recorded
cardinality: once per repository remove

The record that peipkg removed a repository from its configuration and
database, or tried to and failed. Removing a repository that is not
configured succeeds.

Replaces `peipkg.repo-remove`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: object.repository.name          required
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.detail                  optional
  The error as peipkg reported it to the operator. Absent on success.

--- event peipkg.repository.reconfigured
tier: essential
gating: none — every trust-relevant change is recorded
cardinality: once per changed setting

The record that a repository add changed one of an existing repository's
trust-relevant settings. One record for each setting that changed, written
after the add's own `peipkg.repository.added`. The settings are
`signature_policy`, `allow_insecure_transport`, `max_trusted_age_days`,
`min_index_version`, `max_index_staleness_days` and `trust_anchors`.

Only a change made through the flags of `repo add` is seen. An operator who
edits the repository file directly and re-runs the ceremony leaves nothing
to compare.

Replaces `peipkg.config-change`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: object.repository.name          required
field: config.name                     required
  The setting's name as written in the repository file.
field: config.text                     when config.name == signature_policy
field: config.text-previous            when config.name == signature_policy
field: config.value                    optional
  The setting in force, for every setting except `signature_policy`: a
  number of days or an index version as written, 0 for a setting left at
  its default; 1 or 0 for `allow_insecure_transport`; for
  `trust_anchors`, how many anchors are configured, so replacing one
  anchor with another records equal counts.
field: config.value-previous           optional
  The setting it replaced, in the same form as `config.value`.

--- event peipkg.repository.refreshed
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per refresh command

The record that peipkg refreshed the metadata of its configured
repositories. One refresh covers every repository it was asked to; a failure
of one does not stop the others, so a refresh can partly succeed. A refresh
that fails before it reaches any repository writes nothing.

Replaces `peipkg.refresh`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: operation.succeeded-count       required
  Repositories refreshed.
field: operation.failed-count          required
  Repositories whose refresh failed.
field: outcome.success                 required
  False when any repository failed to refresh.

--- event peipkg.transaction.recovered
tier: standard
gating: none
cardinality: once per run of peipkg recover

The record that `peipkg recover` reconciled interrupted transactions,
rolling back each one it found pending, or failed to. Every run writes it,
one that found nothing to recover included. An automatic recovery at the
start of an ordinary operation does not write it.

Replaces `peipkg.recovery`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: operation.succeeded-count       required
  Interrupted transactions rolled back, before the failure on a run that
  failed. Zero when there were none.
field: outcome.success                 required
field: outcome.detail                  optional
  The error, on a run that failed.

--- event peipkg.action.authorised
tier: essential
gating: none — every authorisation is recorded
cardinality: once per elevated action put to the operator, and once per stale repository proceeded with

The record that the operator was asked to authorise an elevated action
specifically, apart from the routine proceed prompt, and what they answered.
`outcome.success` false means they declined. The routine prompt and its
`--yes` never answer one of these. Asked about several actions, the
operator answers them in turn, and the first refusal ends the questions.

`stale-trust-state` and `stale-index` are answered by `--allow-stale`
rather than a prompt, so they are only ever recorded as authorised: without
the flag the command fails instead, and writes no record of this type.

Replaces `peipkg.authorisation`.

field: subject.token.sid               required
field: operation.name                  required
  values: low-trust-provides | foreign-replaces | downgrade | remove-modified-file | stale-trust-state | stale-index
  closed: false
  Which elevated action was put to the operator.
field: object.package.name             optional
  The package the action concerns: the package substituted by
  `provides` for `low-trust-provides`, the successor for
  `foreign-replaces`, the package moving backward for `downgrade`, and the
  package being uninstalled for `remove-modified-file`.
field: object.file.path                when operation.name == remove-modified-file
  The modified file the uninstall would remove, as the package database
  records it: absolute within the root peipkg operated on.
field: object.repository.name          optional
  The repository, for `stale-trust-state` and `stale-index`.
field: outcome.success                 required
  False when the operator declined. For `remove-modified-file`, keeping
  the file and aborting the transaction both decline the removal.
field: outcome.detail                  optional
  The action as it was put to the operator.
